# Security headers for platform-served static assets.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The Flask app set these in an after_request hook, on
# every response, because "a policy applied to some responses is a policy with
# a hole in it" (contex/web/security.py). Here static assets are served by the
# platform without invoking the Worker -- that is what keeps them free and
# unmetered -- so the Worker cannot add headers to them. This file does.
#
# The Worker applies the same set to its own API responses, so the two paths
# together reproduce the original's coverage.
#
# NO NONCE, and that is an improvement rather than a loss. The Flask templates
# carried inline <script> blocks, so the policy needed a per-request nonce to
# avoid 'unsafe-inline'. This frontend has no inline script at all -- app.js is
# an external module -- so 'self' alone is strictly stronger: there is nothing
# for a nonce to authorise, and nothing an injected nonce could forge.
#
# Deliberately absent: COOP/COEP. Cross-origin isolation would buy
# SharedArrayBuffer for multi-threaded ONNX and would break the Firebase Auth
# popup and the gstatic SDK load. See spec R3.

# THE FIREBASE ORIGINS are the same three security.py names, and for the same
# reasons: gstatic serves the browser SDK, apis.google.com serves the sign-in
# iframe, and the project's own authDomain is where the popup completes.
# Flask read the authDomain from config and put it in the policy per request;
# a static file cannot, so it names the pattern instead. NARROW IT to the exact
# authDomain (contex-xxxxx.firebaseapp.com) once the project is fixed -- a host
# pattern is a wider surface than a host, and there is no reason to keep it
# wider than one domain.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' https://www.gstatic.com https://apis.google.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; style-src 'self' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.googleusercontent.com; media-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self' blob: https://www.gstatic.com https://apis.google.com https://*.firebaseapp.com; connect-src 'self' https://identitytoolkit.googleapis.com https://securetoken.googleapis.com https://www.googleapis.com https://*.firebaseapp.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

# The engine, its 9.88 MB format file and the TeX Live tree are content-addressed
# by build: they never change under a given deploy. Without this the format is
# refetched on every compile, which on a phone is the difference between a
# preview and a stall. Static assets are free and unmetered, so caching them
# hard costs nothing and saves the user the bytes.
/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/texmf/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
